# Copy to `.env`. `.env*` is gitignored, so nothing here is ever committed.
#
# Every value below has a working development default *except* DATABASE_URL.
# That asymmetry is deliberate: a missing database fails immediately and
# obviously, whereas a missing secret would otherwise fail subtly, and the
# defaults exist so that a fresh clone runs `next dev` without a scavenger hunt.
# In production, the secrets have no defaults and the process refuses to start.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (required)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The local Postgres runs in Docker. Note the host port is 5433, not 5432 —
# inside the container it is still 5432; only the mapping is moved.
#
#   docker run -d --name ncert-pg \
#     -e POSTGRES_USER=ncert -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ncert -e POSTGRES_DB=ncert \
#     -p 5433:5432 postgres:16
#
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://ncert:ncert@localhost:5433/ncert?schema=public"

# The same database, without the connection pooler in front of it.
#
# Required — Prisma refuses to load a schema whose `env()` is unset, so a
# missing DIRECT_URL breaks `prisma generate`, and therefore the build, before
# it breaks anything else. Locally there is no pooler, so it is the same string.
#
# In production the two differ, and the difference is the whole point:
#
#   DATABASE_URL  the *pooled* URL. Neon: the `-pooler` host. Supabase: port
#                 6543. Vercel Postgres: the value it names POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL.
#                 Every serverless invocation opens a connection; the pooler is
#                 what stops that exhausting the database.
#   DIRECT_URL    the *direct* URL, port 5432, no pooler. Used only by
#                 `prisma migrate deploy` and introspection, which need
#                 session-level state (advisory locks, CREATE TYPE) that a
#                 transaction-mode pooler does not preserve. Run migrations
#                 through the pooled URL and they fail partway with a lock or
#                 prepared-statement error that names neither cause.
#
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://ncert:ncert@localhost:5433/ncert?schema=public"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie. At least 16 characters. Rotating it signs everyone
# out at once, which is the only revocation lever there is — see the note at the
# top of src/lib/session.ts.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Signs OTP challenge tokens and local storage URLs. Both fall back to
# SESSION_SECRET when unset, which is fine for development and worth separating
# in production so that rotating one does not invalidate the other.
OTP_SECRET=""
STORAGE_URL_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "local" (default) writes to .storage/ and serves through /api/dev/storage/.
# "s3" talks to any S3-compatible object store. Anything other than the exact
# string "s3" means local, so a typo fails in development rather than in
# production.
#
# Note that /api/dev/storage/ 404s when NODE_ENV=production. A deployed
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local therefore accepts uploads and hands back links that do
# not resolve: production must be "s3".
STORAGE_DRIVER="local"
# Where the local driver writes. Defaults to ./.storage. Must be gitignored.
STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=""

# --- S3-compatible object storage (required when STORAGE_DRIVER="s3") -------
#
# No provider is named in the code; these five values are the whole
# configuration. Signing is SigV4, hand-written over node:crypto in
# src/lib/s3.ts — no AWS SDK.
#
# Cloudflare R2:  create a bucket, then an R2 API token (Object Read & Write).
#   S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   S3_REGION=auto
# AWS S3:
#   S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com
#   S3_REGION=<region>            # e.g. ap-south-1
# MinIO, for testing:
#   S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9010
#   S3_REGION=us-east-1
#
# The bucket must be PRIVATE. Nothing in this app ever needs public read: every
# object is handed out as a pre-signed URL that expires, and the answer sheets
# in it are children's handwriting.
S3_ENDPOINT=""
S3_REGION="auto"
S3_BUCKET=""
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
# Only for temporary (STS / assumed-role) credentials. Leave unset otherwise.
S3_SESSION_TOKEN=""
# "https://host/bucket/key" (default) vs "https://bucket.host/key". Path style
# is what every S3-compatible server accepts; set "false" only on AWS with a
# bucket that requires virtual-hosted addressing.
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE="true"

# Verify a real endpoint before trusting it with anything:
#   npx tsx scripts/test-s3.ts        # 44 checks, put -> signed GET -> delete

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign-in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is nothing to configure here, which is the point of writing it down.
#
# Two ways in, both server-side only:
#
#   POST /api/auth/register/  + /api/auth/login/   email and a password
#   POST /api/auth/otp/request/ + /api/auth/otp/verify/   a code to a phone
#
# Passwords are scrypt (node:crypto, no dependency), salted per user, and the
# parameters are stored in the hash so they can be raised later. Both paths mint
# the same session cookie, signed with SESSION_SECRET above.
#
# The role a new account gets is STUDENT, always, in production. Outside
# production the sign-in screen offers a role picker so one person can hold a
# student, evaluator, parent and admin account; there is no flag that turns
# that on in production, only NODE_ENV that turns it off.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no SMS provider, and this repo does not add one. In development the
# OTP is deterministic (derived from the phone number) and printed to the server
# log; `GET /api/dev/login/?phone=+91...` returns it directly. In production,
# `deliverOtp()` in src/lib/auth.ts throws until a provider is wired in — a
# sign-in that returns 200 and sends nothing is worse than one that fails.
#
# The OTP flow is still there and still works; email and password exist because
# it cannot let anyone into a production deployment until a provider does.
